/-
# Conjugacy problem in groups of finite-state tree automorphisms (Kourovka Notebook Problem 16.34)

Kourovka Notebook, Problem 16.34 (R. I. Grigorchuk, V. V. Nekrashevich, V. I. Sushchanskiĭ): suppose
that `G` is a finitely generated group acting faithfully on a regular rooted tree by finite-state
automorphisms. Is the conjugacy problem decidable for `G`?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Conj
public import Mathlib.Computability.RE
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.FreeGroup.Basic

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FiniteStateConjugacyProblemKourovka16Problem34Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 16.34 (R. I. Grigorchuk, V. V.
Nekrashevich, V. I. Sushchanskiĭ). UnsolvedMath record UM-00701226 (`KOU-16.34`), dataset
ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

The `d`-regular rooted tree is the set of words over `Fin d`; a tree automorphism is a permutation
preserving lengths and prefixes, and it is finite-state if it has finitely many sections `w ↦ f(u
w)` with the prefix `f(u)` removed (Grigorchuk, Nekrashevich and Sushchanskiĭ). A finitely generated
group acting faithfully by such automorphisms is the subgroup generated by finitely many of them;
its conjugacy problem is `ComputablePred` on pairs of words over the generators, with conjugacy
taken inside the generated subgroup.
-/

/-- **Automorphism of the regular rooted tree.** For $d \in \mathbb{N}$, the vertices of the
$d$-regular rooted tree are the finite words over the alphabet $\{0, \dots, d-1\}$. A
permutation $f$ of this set of words is a tree automorphism when, for all words $u$ and $v$,
writing $|u|$ for the length of $u$ and $uv$ for the concatenation of $u$ and $v$, the word
$f(u)$ has length $|u|$ and the first $|u|$ letters of $f(uv)$ form $f(u)$. -/
def IsTreeAut {d : ℕ} (f : Equiv.Perm (List (Fin d))) : Prop :=
  ∀ u v : List (Fin d), (f u).length = u.length ∧ (f (u ++ v)).take u.length = f u

/-- **Section of a map of the regular rooted tree at a vertex.** For $d \in \mathbb{N}$, let
$f$ be a permutation of the finite words over $\{0, \dots, d-1\}$ (the vertices of the
$d$-regular rooted tree) and let $u$ be a word, of length $|u|$. The section (state) of $f$ at
$u$ is the map sending each word $w$ to the word obtained from $f(uw)$ by deleting its first
$|u|$ letters, where $uw$ is the concatenation of $u$ and $w$. -/
def treeSection {d : ℕ} (f : Equiv.Perm (List (Fin d))) (u : List (Fin d)) :
    List (Fin d) → List (Fin d) :=
  fun w => (f (u ++ w)).drop u.length

/-- **Finite-state automorphism of the regular rooted tree.** For $d \in \mathbb{N}$, a
permutation $f$ of the finite words over $\{0, \dots, d-1\}$ is a finite-state automorphism of
the $d$-regular rooted tree when it is an automorphism of the tree and the sections of $f$ at
all vertices $u$ form a finite set of maps. -/
def IsFiniteStateAut {d : ℕ} (f : Equiv.Perm (List (Fin d))) : Prop :=
  IsTreeAut f ∧ (Set.range (treeSection f)).Finite

/-- **Value of a word in the generators.** For $d, k \in \mathbb{N}$, let $g_0, \dots, g_{k-1}$
be permutations of the finite words over $\{0, \dots, d-1\}$, and let $w$ be a word whose
letters are pairs $(i, b)$ with $i < k$ and $b$ a boolean. The value of $w$ is the element of
the group generated by $g_0, \dots, g_{k-1}$ obtained by multiplying, from left to right, $g_i$
for each letter $(i, \mathrm{true})$ and $g_i^{-1}$ for each letter $(i, \mathrm{false})$. -/
def wordValue {d k : ℕ} (gens : Fin k → Equiv.Perm (List (Fin d))) (w : List (Fin k × Bool)) :
    Subgroup.closure (Set.range gens) :=
  FreeGroup.lift (fun i => ⟨gens i, Subgroup.subset_closure (Set.mem_range_self i)⟩)
    (FreeGroup.mk w)

/-- **Kourovka Problem 16.34: conjugacy in finite-state automorphism groups.** The proposition
that every finitely generated group of finite-state automorphisms of a regular rooted tree has
decidable conjugacy problem: for all $d, k \in \mathbb{N}$ and all finite-state automorphisms
$g_0, \dots, g_{k-1}$ of the $d$-regular rooted tree, some algorithm decides, given two words
in the $g_i$ and their inverses, whether the elements they spell are conjugate in the group
generated by $g_0, \dots, g_{k-1}$.

The problem was posed by R. I. Grigorchuk, V. V. Nekrashevich and V. I. Sushchanskiĭ. -/
def finiteStateConjugacyDecidable : Prop :=
  ∀ (d k : ℕ) (gens : Fin k → Equiv.Perm (List (Fin d))), (∀ i, IsFiniteStateAut (gens i)) →
    ComputablePred fun uv : List (Fin k × Bool) × List (Fin k × Bool) =>
      IsConj (wordValue gens uv.1) (wordValue gens uv.2)

/-- **Finite-state tree automorphism groups with undecidable conjugacy problem.** The answer
to Kourovka Problem 16.34 is negative: there exist $d, k \in \mathbb{N}$ and finite-state
automorphisms $g_0, \dots, g_{k-1}$ of the $d$-regular rooted tree such that no algorithm
decides, given two words in the $g_i$ and their inverses, whether the elements they spell are
conjugate in the group generated by $g_0, \dots, g_{k-1}$.

Šunić and Ventura (Theorem 1.1) construct automaton groups, finitely generated groups
generated by finite self-similar sets of finite-state automorphisms of a regular rooted tree,
with unsolvable conjugacy problem; the Kourovka Notebook (21st issue) answers 16.34 "No, not
always".

Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The
Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 16.34 (R. I. Grigorchuk, V. V.
Nekrashevich, V. I. Sushchanskiĭ); resolution: Z. Šunić and E. Ventura, The conjugacy problem in
automaton groups is not solvable, J. Algebra 364 (2012), 148-154, arXiv:1010.1993, Theorem 1.1
(there exist automaton groups with unsolvable conjugacy problem; the examples are (free
abelian)-by-free groups generated by finite self-similar sets of tree automorphisms); the Kourovka
Notebook (21st issue) marks 16.34 solved (No, not always), https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.1993.
-/
public theorem_wanted finiteStateConjugacyDecidable_refuted : ¬ finiteStateConjugacyDecidable

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FiniteStateConjugacyProblemKourovka16Problem34Wanted
